    <h1>A workshop, not a factory</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Cacao &amp; Crumb started with a borrowed melanger and a sack of Ecuadorian
      beans. Six years later we still make every bar in the same room.
    </p>

    <article class="prose">
      <h2>The short version</h2>
      <p>
        We are three people: a chocolate maker, a roaster, and whoever is answering
        email that day. We buy cacao directly from four farms, roast in small lots, and
        grind for two days. The bars are tempered, molded, wrapped, and stamped by hand.
      </p>
      <p>
        We do not use emulsifiers, vanilla flavoring, or "natural flavors." If a bar
        tastes like cherry, that is because the bean tastes like cherry.
      </p>

      <h2>What we care about</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Paying for the work.</strong> We publish what we pay per kilo and
          buy at least two tonnes per origin each year.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Less packaging.</strong> Compostable wrappers, paper tape, and no
          plastic inserts. The box is the shipping box.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Telling the truth about batch size.</strong> When we run out, we say
          so rather than blending in beans from elsewhere.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </article>

